1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:02,530
Let's try an experiment.

2
00:00:02,780 --> 00:00:05,190
You open the door, and you step outside.

3
00:00:05,480 --> 00:00:07,670
It's minus fifty degrees Celsius.

4
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:10,210
The air is perfectly still.

5
00:00:11,342 --> 00:00:16,352
In the first second, your breath turns
into a cloud of tiny ice crystals.

6
00:00:17,022 --> 00:00:20,992
Within seconds, frost starts
growing on your eyelashes.

7
00:00:21,402 --> 00:00:26,642
Every breath stings, because your nose has
to warm air that's nearly ninety degrees

8
00:00:26,682 --> 00:00:28,492
colder than your body.

9
00:00:29,774 --> 00:00:31,524
Now the clock is running.

10
00:00:32,014 --> 00:00:34,724
At this temperature, in even a light wind,

11
00:00:34,974 --> 00:00:38,684
bare skin on your face can
freeze in two to five minutes.

12
00:00:39,114 --> 00:00:43,004
That's why nobody here goes
out with an uncovered face.

13
00:00:44,354 --> 00:00:47,924
And if you throw a cup of
boiling water into the air,

14
00:00:48,034 --> 00:00:50,424
it never hits the ground as water.

15
00:00:50,794 --> 00:00:53,224
It vanishes into a cloud of snow.

16
00:00:54,444 --> 00:00:56,814
Now here's the strange part.

17
00:00:57,564 --> 00:01:00,964
Three hundred and fifty-five
thousand people live in this cold,

18
00:01:01,004 --> 00:01:02,774
every single winter.

19
00:01:03,064 --> 00:01:03,954
They go to work.

20
00:01:04,384 --> 00:01:06,534
Most days, their kids go to school.

21
00:01:06,744 --> 00:01:09,554
And inside their apartments,
it's plus twenty.

22
00:01:11,069 --> 00:01:14,199
This is Yakutsk, in eastern Siberia.

23
00:01:14,849 --> 00:01:18,619
An average January day
here is minus thirty-seven.

24
00:01:19,129 --> 00:01:24,119
If you live in London, that's forty-two
degrees colder than your January.

25
00:01:24,409 --> 00:01:27,759
It is the city with the
coldest winters on Earth.

26
00:01:29,134 --> 00:01:32,134
So how do you keep a whole
city warm, and running,

27
00:01:32,174 --> 00:01:33,524
at minus fifty?

28
00:01:33,934 --> 00:01:35,504
It takes a lot of tricks.

29
00:01:36,014 --> 00:01:37,304
We've counted seven.

30
00:01:37,534 --> 00:01:40,764
And the most important one
is the one you'd never guess.

31
00:01:41,034 --> 00:01:43,704
It's hiding under every
building in the city.

32
00:01:46,622 --> 00:01:48,612
But first, a fair question.

33
00:01:49,122 --> 00:01:51,212
Why would anyone live here at all?

34
00:01:51,482 --> 00:01:55,902
Yakutsk is the capital of Yakutia,
a region bigger than Argentina,

35
00:01:55,942 --> 00:01:59,032
and one of the richest in
the world under the ground.

36
00:01:59,602 --> 00:02:04,632
The diamond company that works here mines
about ninety percent of Russia's diamonds,

37
00:02:04,882 --> 00:02:08,792
and Russia produces about a third
of all the diamonds on Earth.

38
00:02:09,342 --> 00:02:11,032
And people keep coming.

39
00:02:11,582 --> 00:02:13,532
Between twenty ten and twenty twenty-one,

40
00:02:13,642 --> 00:02:17,472
the population of Yakutsk
grew by almost a third.

41
00:02:17,962 --> 00:02:23,092
In the coldest big city on the planet,
they're still building new apartment blocks.

42
00:02:26,319 --> 00:02:28,549
Let's start with the easiest trick.

43
00:02:28,759 --> 00:02:32,689
In Yakutsk, from November to March,
you don't really need a freezer.

44
00:02:33,099 --> 00:02:34,629
The whole city is one.

45
00:02:35,190 --> 00:02:39,260
At the open-air market,
fish is sold frozen solid,

46
00:02:39,330 --> 00:02:43,340
stacked like firewood, with
no refrigerators at all.

47
00:02:43,690 --> 00:02:48,610
The traders stand outside all day,
wrapped in so many layers that one of them

48
00:02:48,650 --> 00:02:51,280
described it as dressing like a cabbage.

49
00:02:52,685 --> 00:02:55,115
And the cold even shapes the food.

50
00:02:55,565 --> 00:02:59,255
A local favorite is stroganina: raw fish,

51
00:02:59,385 --> 00:03:04,515
frozen hard, then shaved into thin
curls and eaten before it thaws.

52
00:03:05,782 --> 00:03:08,912
Just outside the city, there's
an even bigger freezer.

53
00:03:09,442 --> 00:03:14,182
Old tunnels dug straight into the frozen
ground have been turned into an attraction

54
00:03:14,222 --> 00:03:16,292
called the Permafrost Kingdom.

55
00:03:16,782 --> 00:03:18,692
It opened in two thousand five.

56
00:03:19,282 --> 00:03:22,512
The walls are frozen earth,
the sculptures are ice,

57
00:03:22,662 --> 00:03:26,212
and it stays below zero
all year, even in July.

58
00:03:27,438 --> 00:03:28,408
Quick question.

59
00:03:28,558 --> 00:03:31,968
Is Yakutsk the coldest city
on Earth, all year round?

60
00:03:32,198 --> 00:03:32,908
Not quite.

61
00:03:33,298 --> 00:03:36,228
Over a whole year, Norilsk,
far to the northwest,

62
00:03:36,378 --> 00:03:38,968
is very slightly colder on average.

63
00:03:39,298 --> 00:03:42,988
But no city of this size
has winters like Yakutsk.

64
00:03:43,258 --> 00:03:47,488
Here, December and January both
average around minus thirty-seven.

65
00:03:48,242 --> 00:03:50,572
Here's another one you
may have seen in the news.

66
00:03:51,022 --> 00:03:55,742
In January twenty twenty-three, headlines
said Yakutsk had hit minus sixty-two point

67
00:03:55,782 --> 00:03:56,432
seven.

68
00:03:56,802 --> 00:03:57,592
It didn't.

69
00:03:57,722 --> 00:04:00,972
That reading came from a weather
station elsewhere in Yakutia.

70
00:04:01,262 --> 00:04:04,412
In Yakutsk itself, it was
"only" about minus fifty.

71
00:04:04,842 --> 00:04:08,452
The city's own record is
minus sixty-four point four,

72
00:04:08,502 --> 00:04:10,252
set back in eighteen ninety-one.

73
00:04:10,790 --> 00:04:15,280
And the famous minus seventy-one
in the village of Oymyakon?

74
00:04:15,350 --> 00:04:18,840
That number is an old,
unofficial estimate.

75
00:04:19,230 --> 00:04:23,210
The official record there is
minus sixty-seven point seven,

76
00:04:23,250 --> 00:04:24,880
from nineteen thirty-three.

77
00:04:25,690 --> 00:04:29,980
Still, a long way below anything
most of us will ever feel.

78
00:04:30,503 --> 00:04:32,933
That leads to the first big problem.

79
00:04:33,243 --> 00:04:36,013
How do you heat a city
that cold, for that long?

80
00:04:38,730 --> 00:04:42,160
In most of the world, every
house has its own boiler.

81
00:04:42,630 --> 00:04:44,320
Yakutsk does it differently.

82
00:04:44,930 --> 00:04:49,640
Big power stations burn natural gas,
and they don't just make electricity.

83
00:04:50,230 --> 00:04:55,030
They heat water, and pump it across the
city to the radiators in every apartment

84
00:04:55,070 --> 00:04:55,700
block.

85
00:04:55,910 --> 00:04:57,420
It's called district heating.

86
00:04:58,304 --> 00:05:01,314
The newest of them, opened
in twenty seventeen,

87
00:05:01,384 --> 00:05:05,954
can send out enough heat for a
large part of the city on its own.

88
00:05:06,424 --> 00:05:11,574
The gas comes by pipeline from
fields hundreds of kilometers away.

89
00:05:12,862 --> 00:05:14,392
Think about what that means.

90
00:05:14,682 --> 00:05:17,512
Your apartment isn't
heated by your own boiler.

91
00:05:17,882 --> 00:05:22,332
It's heated by water that was hot
in a power station kilometers away,

92
00:05:22,462 --> 00:05:25,582
pushed through insulated
pipes across a frozen city,

93
00:05:25,622 --> 00:05:27,712
all winter, without stopping.

94
00:05:28,182 --> 00:05:31,572
And if that hot water ever
stopped moving in deep winter,

95
00:05:31,762 --> 00:05:34,692
the pipes themselves would
be in danger of freezing.

96
00:05:35,288 --> 00:05:37,418
And the heating season is long.

97
00:05:37,788 --> 00:05:43,528
By law, the radiators come on after five
days in a row averaging plus eight or

98
00:05:43,568 --> 00:05:44,298
colder.

99
00:05:44,828 --> 00:05:48,458
In Yakutsk, that usually
happens in early September.

100
00:05:49,008 --> 00:05:51,518
They stay on until
around the middle of May.

101
00:05:51,888 --> 00:05:53,608
That's more than eight months of heating,

102
00:05:53,648 --> 00:05:54,758
every year.

103
00:05:55,926 --> 00:05:57,876
There's even a legal minimum.

104
00:05:58,226 --> 00:06:03,366
In regions this cold, a living room is
supposed to stay at least at plus twenty

105
00:06:03,406 --> 00:06:04,036
degrees.

106
00:06:04,646 --> 00:06:06,476
Outside, minus fifty.

107
00:06:06,946 --> 00:06:08,546
Inside, plus twenty.

108
00:06:08,586 --> 00:06:12,816
Seventy degrees of difference,
held back by a wall and a window.

109
00:06:14,191 --> 00:06:17,021
But a system like this has one weak point:

110
00:06:17,171 --> 00:06:17,861
it's shared.

111
00:06:18,311 --> 00:06:23,151
In October twenty seventeen, a fire at
one of the city's power stations cut

112
00:06:23,191 --> 00:06:28,101
electricity and water across Yakutsk,
and the region declared an emergency.

113
00:06:28,791 --> 00:06:30,231
It was fixed within a day.

114
00:06:30,271 --> 00:06:31,941
And it was still autumn.

115
00:06:32,251 --> 00:06:34,981
Now imagine the same thing in January.

116
00:06:37,436 --> 00:06:40,716
Walk past a car park
in Yakutsk in January,

117
00:06:40,756 --> 00:06:43,066
and you'll notice something strange.

118
00:06:43,736 --> 00:06:47,306
Many of the cars are
running, with nobody inside.

119
00:06:47,596 --> 00:06:49,006
Sometimes for hours.

120
00:06:49,336 --> 00:06:50,686
Sometimes all night.

121
00:06:51,973 --> 00:06:53,283
It's not laziness.

122
00:06:53,733 --> 00:06:57,263
At minus fifty, engine oil
thickens, batteries lose power,

123
00:06:57,313 --> 00:07:00,463
and a car that's switched off
may simply refuse to start again.

124
00:07:00,873 --> 00:07:04,043
So people keep the engine
running, park in heated garages,

125
00:07:04,133 --> 00:07:05,683
or plug in engine heaters.

126
00:07:05,873 --> 00:07:07,723
Even the fuel has to be special.

127
00:07:08,233 --> 00:07:10,593
Ordinary diesel turns
to jelly in this cold,

128
00:07:10,633 --> 00:07:14,763
so the north uses arctic diesel,
made to keep flowing far below zero.

129
00:07:15,434 --> 00:07:15,394
Remember the boiling
water that turned to snow?

130
00:07:15,434 --> 00:07:16,844
Thousands of running
cars do the same thing.

131
00:07:17,154 --> 00:07:19,184
Their exhaust is full of warm water vapor.

132
00:07:19,334 --> 00:07:22,834
But air at minus forty can hold about two
hundred times less water vapor than the

133
00:07:22,874 --> 00:07:23,944
air in a warm room.

134
00:07:24,314 --> 00:07:28,464
So the vapor freezes instantly into tiny
ice crystals that hang over the streets.

135
00:07:29,007 --> 00:07:30,577
This is ice fog.

136
00:07:30,867 --> 00:07:33,267
On the coldest days, it fills the streets,

137
00:07:33,307 --> 00:07:35,677
and visibility drops sharply.

138
00:07:36,107 --> 00:07:40,597
The city, quite literally, disappears
into a cloud it makes itself.

139
00:07:43,774 --> 00:07:47,264
So when does Yakutsk
finally say: it's too cold?

140
00:07:47,514 --> 00:07:49,904
For schools, there's an actual rule.

141
00:07:50,374 --> 00:07:54,924
In recent winters, when it's around
minus forty-five with no wind,

142
00:07:55,134 --> 00:07:57,124
the youngest children stay home.

143
00:07:57,494 --> 00:08:01,624
At minus fifty, every grade
switches to learning from home.

144
00:08:02,904 --> 00:08:06,814
Which means that at minus
forty, minus forty-two,

145
00:08:07,304 --> 00:08:10,074
minus forty-four, school is open.

146
00:08:10,704 --> 00:08:15,824
Kids walk there in the dark, because in
December the sun is up for only about five

147
00:08:15,864 --> 00:08:16,974
hours a day.

148
00:08:18,267 --> 00:08:20,577
And then, in June, the opposite.

149
00:08:21,247 --> 00:08:26,417
The sun is up for about twenty hours,
and the nights never get properly dark.

150
00:08:26,847 --> 00:08:31,077
Yakutsk lives half the year in the
dark and half the year in the light.

151
00:08:34,168 --> 00:08:37,698
Here's a fact that
surprises almost everyone.

152
00:08:38,248 --> 00:08:42,498
Yakutsk sits on the Lena, one of
the biggest rivers in the world.

153
00:08:42,628 --> 00:08:44,878
And there is no bridge across it.

154
00:08:44,968 --> 00:08:47,958
Not here, and not anywhere
in the whole region.

155
00:08:49,446 --> 00:08:52,176
In summer, cars cross by ferry.

156
00:08:52,646 --> 00:08:56,756
In winter, the river freezes
so hard that it becomes a road,

157
00:08:56,906 --> 00:08:59,496
and trucks drive straight across the ice.

158
00:09:00,666 --> 00:09:02,876
But twice a year, neither works.

159
00:09:03,286 --> 00:09:07,356
In autumn, the ice is forming
but too thin to drive on.

160
00:09:07,566 --> 00:09:09,676
In spring, it's breaking up.

161
00:09:10,026 --> 00:09:13,906
For weeks at a time, the main road
link from the other bank is cut,

162
00:09:13,946 --> 00:09:17,036
and only hovercraft and helicopters cross.

163
00:09:18,443 --> 00:09:23,513
In twenty twenty-three, for example,
the last ferries ran in late October.

164
00:09:24,043 --> 00:09:26,963
The next ones only started
again at the end of May,

165
00:09:27,003 --> 00:09:29,193
after the ice had broken up.

166
00:09:29,563 --> 00:09:34,963
In between, the city lived on the ice
road and on whatever had been delivered by

167
00:09:35,003 --> 00:09:36,453
river during the summer.

168
00:09:37,032 --> 00:09:40,042
That's why a bridge is
finally being built:

169
00:09:40,212 --> 00:09:44,452
a cable-stayed crossing about three
kilometers long over the river,

170
00:09:44,492 --> 00:09:48,082
and more than fourteen
kilometers with its approaches.

171
00:09:48,692 --> 00:09:53,162
Its piers are being sunk on piles
more than forty meters long.

172
00:09:53,552 --> 00:09:56,502
The current target is the
end of twenty twenty-eight.

173
00:09:58,901 --> 00:10:02,061
Look closely at the streets
of a city like Yakutsk,

174
00:10:02,101 --> 00:10:05,051
and you'll see pipes
that most cities hide.

175
00:10:05,721 --> 00:10:11,031
Thick, wrapped pipes carrying hot water,
running along the streets above the ground.

176
00:10:12,464 --> 00:10:14,914
In a normal city, you bury your pipes.

177
00:10:15,224 --> 00:10:17,154
Here, that creates a problem.

178
00:10:17,584 --> 00:10:21,064
A pipe full of hot water,
buried in frozen ground,

179
00:10:21,104 --> 00:10:22,494
acts like a heater.

180
00:10:22,824 --> 00:10:26,294
It melts the ice around it,
and the ground starts to sink.

181
00:10:26,604 --> 00:10:30,654
So the pipes go up, onto supports,
away from the frozen earth.

182
00:10:31,920 --> 00:10:35,370
And that's a clue to the last,
and most important trick.

183
00:10:35,600 --> 00:10:39,870
Because in Yakutsk, the real
danger isn't the cold air.

184
00:10:40,080 --> 00:10:41,930
It's what lies under your feet.

185
00:10:44,921 --> 00:10:51,011
In eighteen twenty-eight, a merchant in
Yakutsk named Fyodor Shergin wanted a well.

186
00:10:51,361 --> 00:10:54,891
He ran the local office of
a Russian trading company,

187
00:10:55,081 --> 00:10:56,331
and he needed water.

188
00:10:56,721 --> 00:10:58,011
So he started digging.

189
00:10:59,437 --> 00:11:00,777
He dug, and he dug.

190
00:11:00,817 --> 00:11:02,207
For nine years.

191
00:11:02,697 --> 00:11:07,377
By eighteen thirty-seven, his shaft
was a hundred and sixteen meters deep,

192
00:11:07,417 --> 00:11:11,407
and he still hadn't found a
single drop of liquid water.

193
00:11:11,697 --> 00:11:13,507
Every meter of it was frozen.

194
00:11:13,997 --> 00:11:14,847
He gave up.

195
00:11:16,097 --> 00:11:21,137
A few years later, a scientist named
Alexander von Middendorff measured the

196
00:11:21,177 --> 00:11:23,827
temperature inside Shergin's shaft.

197
00:11:24,357 --> 00:11:26,967
It was below zero all the way down.

198
00:11:27,297 --> 00:11:32,217
He estimated that the frozen ground under
Yakutsk went down almost two hundred

199
00:11:32,257 --> 00:11:32,907
meters.

200
00:11:33,477 --> 00:11:36,167
Today we know it's
hundreds of meters thick.

201
00:11:36,722 --> 00:11:40,452
This is permafrost, and the
whole city stands on it.

202
00:11:40,802 --> 00:11:42,732
Frozen, it's as hard as rock.

203
00:11:43,242 --> 00:11:45,372
Thawed, a lot of it turns into mud.

204
00:11:45,762 --> 00:11:49,342
So if you put a warm apartment
block straight on top of it,

205
00:11:49,382 --> 00:11:53,852
the heat flows down, the ice melts,
and the building sinks and cracks.

206
00:11:55,173 --> 00:11:59,893
That's why the apartment blocks of
Yakutsk stand on concrete piles,

207
00:11:59,933 --> 00:12:02,223
with an open gap underneath.

208
00:12:02,793 --> 00:12:05,763
The gap isn't there to keep
the cold out of the building.

209
00:12:06,093 --> 00:12:06,983
It's the opposite.

210
00:12:07,553 --> 00:12:10,113
It keeps the building's
heat away from the ground,

211
00:12:10,153 --> 00:12:12,803
so the ice underneath stays frozen.

212
00:12:13,726 --> 00:12:15,196
So here's the twist.

213
00:12:15,626 --> 00:12:18,816
Everything we've seen so
far, the power stations,

214
00:12:19,046 --> 00:12:22,816
the heating, the running cars,
all of it fights the cold.

215
00:12:23,266 --> 00:12:25,856
But this last trick protects the cold.

216
00:12:26,426 --> 00:12:31,736
Yakutsk can only stay warm on the inside
if the ground under it stays frozen.

217
00:12:33,190 --> 00:12:34,840
And the ground is warming.

218
00:12:35,290 --> 00:12:40,810
At Shergin's shaft, scientists have been
measuring the frozen ground for almost a

219
00:12:40,850 --> 00:12:41,460
century.

220
00:12:41,990 --> 00:12:46,790
Ten meters down, it's about three degrees
warmer than it was in the nineteen

221
00:12:46,830 --> 00:12:47,660
thirties.

222
00:12:48,831 --> 00:12:55,391
Over one decade, accidents to buildings
caused by thawing ground in Yakutsk rose by

223
00:12:55,431 --> 00:12:57,021
sixty-one percent.

224
00:12:57,311 --> 00:13:02,171
And satellite measurements show parts
of the city sinking by more than four

225
00:13:02,211 --> 00:13:03,621
centimeters a year.

226
00:13:04,868 --> 00:13:08,398
The coldest winters on Earth
are not the city's enemy.

227
00:13:08,788 --> 00:13:09,898
They're its foundation.

228
00:13:12,751 --> 00:13:14,921
So let's go back to our experiment.

229
00:13:15,251 --> 00:13:16,321
It's minus fifty.

230
00:13:16,751 --> 00:13:18,621
You've been outside long enough.

231
00:13:18,791 --> 00:13:23,041
You walk back to your building, past
the running cars and the ice fog,

232
00:13:23,191 --> 00:13:24,381
and you open the door.

233
00:13:24,831 --> 00:13:25,581
Plus twenty.

234
00:13:27,005 --> 00:13:29,835
Now you know what's holding
that temperature up:

235
00:13:30,065 --> 00:13:34,335
a power station burning gas from
hundreds of kilometers away,

236
00:13:34,645 --> 00:13:39,985
pipes lifted off the ground, and concrete
piles standing in ground that was frozen

237
00:13:40,025 --> 00:13:42,235
long before the city existed.

238
00:13:42,865 --> 00:13:47,855
A city that survives the cold by keeping
the ground beneath it colder still.

239
00:13:48,352 --> 00:13:49,862
And one last thing.

240
00:13:50,172 --> 00:13:53,162
In summer, Yakutsk can
hit plus thirty-eight.

241
00:13:53,652 --> 00:13:57,142
That's almost as hot as
London's hottest day ever.

242
00:13:57,392 --> 00:14:00,422
From its coldest day on
record to its hottest,

243
00:14:00,652 --> 00:14:02,082
a hundred and three degrees.

244
00:14:03,631 --> 00:14:06,711
So, would you survive a winter in Yakutsk?

245
00:14:06,751 --> 00:14:09,681
And which part would be
hardest for you: the cold,

246
00:14:09,831 --> 00:14:12,901
the dark, or the car that never turns off?

247
00:14:13,171 --> 00:14:14,401
Tell me in the comments.

248
00:14:15,111 --> 00:14:16,581
This was How It's Run.
